Step-by-Step Audio Guided Tour: Picasso, Miro, Dali: Unbound exhibition at MFA, Boston, MA - Museum Tour
Tour-Category: museum

Stop 1: Le Lézard aux plumes d'or

Address: 465 Huntington Ave, Boston, MA 02115

Coordinates: 42.339444, -71.094167

Orientation: You are about to explore the Picasso, Miro, Dali: Unbound exhibition at the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston. This exhibition showcases the intriguing transformation of artistic expression through the lens of the livre d'artiste, or artist's book, a medium that allows artists to experiment with the convergence of visual art and literature. Within this collection, you will encounter three pivotal works: Le Lézard aux plumes d'or, Moses and Monotheism, and Au Soleil du Plafond. Each piece reflects the unique interpretations and stylistic evolutions of these iconic artists. Organized by the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, this exhibition invites you to delve into the transformative power of these creations, on view through March 2024, offering a rare glimpse into the dynamic interplay of text and image that defined an era. In the stops ahead, you will discover the revolutionary theory that Moses was originally an Egyptian priest at Moses and Monotheism and the extraordinary confluence of artistic vision and literary insight in "Au Soleil du Plafond." Your first stop is Le Lézard aux plumes d'or. With its vivid colors and fantastical shapes, "Le Lézard aux plumes d'or" by Joan Miró draws you into a world where the mundane transforms into the extraordinary. Stand slightly to the right to fully immerse yourself in the swirling forms that seem to leap off the page. This angle lets you appreciate the richness of each lithograph, printed with meticulous detail in every line and hue.

The work exemplifies the exhibition's thesis that the book itself can be a revolutionary art form. Tériade, known for publishing such ambitious projects, ensured that the interplay of images and words would become a hallmark of the genre. The vision and Tériade's execution, "Le Lézard aux plumes d'or" emerges not just as a book, but as a bold artistic statement that pushes the boundaries of how stories and illustrations intertwine. As visitors descend into this enchanted world, they glimpse the potential of art to reveal deeper truths through transformation.


Directions: Continue through Museum of Fine Arts, Boston — next is Moses and Monotheism.



Stop 2: Moses and Monotheism

Address: 465 Huntington Ave, Boston, MA 02115

Coordinates: 42.339444, -71.094167

Orientation: Stand near the center of the gallery, facing the display that houses Salvador Dalí’s "Moses and Monotheism." From this vantage point, one can appreciate the intricate illustrations within the book and their symbiotic relationship with the text.

Decades later, Salvador Dalí transformed Freud's text into a visual masterpiece by illustrating it, creating a livre d'artiste, where art and literature converge into a single entity. Dalí's meticulous use of lithographs, combined with the book's precise binding, exemplifies this fusion, as images and words unite on the page. Similarly, Miró's collaboration with Tériade in 1958 to craft the "À toute épreuve" book with woodcuts reflects this collaborative spirit, adding depth to the exhibit's narrative of artistic synergy. This approach advanced the exhibition’s argument that artists like Dalí and Miró revolutionized the book as an art form through their intricate collaborations. These artists used light, shadow, and composition to explore themes of transformation, much like how the sun itself has been depicted as a transformative force throughout art history.


Directions: Your final stop in Museum of Fine Arts, Boston: Au Soleil du Plafond.



Stop 3: Au Soleil du Plafond

Address: 465 Huntington Ave, Boston, MA 02115

Coordinates: 42.339444, -71.094167

Orientation: Look for Au Soleil du Plafond in the galleries.

Published by Tériade in 1955, "Au Soleil du Plafond" is not merely a book but an extraordinary confluence of artistic vision and literary insight. Freud's philosophies, which often intersect with Surrealism, further enrich the work's intellectual backdrop. The result exemplifies the exhibition's thesis: that the art of bookmaking can transcend the page, becoming a space where image, word, and typography coalesce in a new realm of artistic expression. This is not just a book but a complex interplay of visual and intellectual elements, challenging our perception of what an art piece can be.



The exhibition is on view through March 2024. That's 3 stops — Le Lézard aux plumes d'or, a bold artistic statement by Tériade and Au Soleil du Plafond, published by Tériade in 1955 as an extraordinary confluence. Closing: The Treat Page shows whether there are real savings at local shops and restaurants around here. We can also generate news articles for you to listen to on the way back.

